12. GCP Sandbox IAM Limitation
A restricted GCP sandbox may prevent the active cloud_user account from modifying the project's IAM policy.

For example, a command such as:
gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding ...

may fail with a Policy update access denied error when the active identity does not have:
resourcemanager.projects.setIamPolicy
permission.

There is no supported CLI command that bypasses this IAM boundary.

The correct approach is to use an authorized sandbox administrator or identity with sufficient IAM authority to grant the required roles.

-------------------------------------------------------------

12.1 GKE IAM and Kubernetes RBAC

GKE authorization can involve both:
•	Google Cloud IAM
•	Kubernetes RBAC

A Kubernetes ClusterRoleBinding does not necessarily grant a missing Google Cloud IAM permission.

For example, if GKE reports that:
container.nodes.list

is required, the identity used by the operation must have a Google Cloud IAM role containing that permission.

A Kubernetes RBAC binding alone may not resolve a GKE IAM authorization failure.

A test RBAC binding used during troubleshooting was:

kubectl create clusterrolebinding cloud-user-node-view \
  --clusterrole=view \
  --user=<KUBERNETES_USER_ID>

This should be treated as a troubleshooting/test configuration rather than a production authorization design.

